1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,443
What if a fee that's only one percent
took more money than you ever put in?

2
00:00:04,343 --> 00:00:09,052
Two identical funds. Same investments.
One charges one percent a year more.
That's it.

3
00:00:09,902 --> 00:00:14,149
You put in five hundred bucks a month
for forty years. Two hundred forty
grand total.

4
00:00:14,449 --> 00:00:18,081
Say both earn seven percent before
fees. Not a promise, just math.

5
00:00:18,981 --> 00:00:22,748
After ten years, the gap's like forty-
six hundred bucks. Whatever, right?

6
00:00:23,048 --> 00:00:24,935
Twenty years: twenty-nine thousand.

7
00:00:25,135 --> 00:00:27,214
Thirty years: a hundred seven
thousand.

8
00:00:28,014 --> 00:00:33,382
Forty years? Fund A hits one point
three— one point three one million.
Fund B: nine ninety-five thousand.

9
00:00:33,682 --> 00:00:36,975
That one percent fee cost you three
hundred sixteen thousand dollars.

10
00:00:37,275 --> 00:00:40,751
Wait. That's more than the two forty
you put in.

11
00:00:41,051 --> 00:00:44,238
One percent a year ended up as twenty-
four percent of the whole pot.

12
00:00:45,138 --> 00:00:47,816
So would you still call one percent
small? Comment.

13
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:49,852
Follow for the next one.
